        {"question":"Scan output for a host you own:\nPORT     STATE  SERVICE\n22/tcp   open   ssh\n3306/tcp filtered mysql\nWhat is the correct interpretation?", "choices":["SSH is reachable; MySQL is blocked or dropped by a firewall, so Nmap cannot tell if it is listening", "MySQL is open and unprotected", "SSH is closed", "Filtered means the host is offline"], "answer":0, "explain":"Open means the service connected. Filtered means no response was received (likely firewall drop), so the state is unknown, not open."},
        {"question":"Which scan choice is the least disruptive while still useful for an authorised internal lab inventory?", "choices":["A full -p- scan with aggressive timing on production hosts", "A targeted -sV scan of known ports on lab hosts you own, during an agreed window", "An OS-detection scan of the public internet", "Scanning with spoofed sources to hide your activity"], "answer":1, "explain":"Narrow scope, known ports, and an agreed window keep the scan useful and low-impact. Broad, aggressive scans risk disruption and leave scope unclear."},
