        {"question":"Response headers from a site:\nStrict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload\nContent-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'\nWhat is the correct review conclusion?", "choices":["Both headers are absent and should be added", "HSTS is strong (includes preload and includeSubDomains); CSP restricts scripts to the same origin, so review inline/external scripts against it", "CSP means the site is only served over HTTP", "HSTS preload alone protects against SQL injection"], "answer":1, "explain":"HSTS with includeSubDomains and preload is the strong form. A CSP of default-src 'self' with script-src 'self' blocks inline and third-party scripts."},
        {"question":"Config review: a server is believed to be forcing HTTPS, but http://site.example redirects fine. Which check confirms the TLS upgrade is actually enforced for everyone?", "choices":["Check that the certificate exists", "Request the http:// URL and confirm a redirect to https://, and check the HSTS header on the HTTPS response", "Check the DNS A record", "Ping the server"], "answer":1, "explain":"Enforcement needs a redirect from HTTP to HTTPS plus an HSTS header so browsers keep using HTTPS. A certificate alone does not force HTTPS."},
        {"question":"A login page returns 401 for an unauthenticated request and 200 after valid login. An auditor writes '401 means the page is forbidden.' What is the mistake?", "choices":["There is no mistake", "401 means authentication is required; 403 means authenticated/identified but forbidden. The distinction affects whether retrying with credentials helps", "401 always means the server is down", "401 and 403 are identical"], "answer":1, "explain":"401 = authentication required. 403 = access refused even though the request was understood. Mixing them up misdirects remediation."},
